{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794156981,"nonce":"Gx7S5CCvlfMJujhNX2WZ2MauiuA=","message":"2026-11-08, iPhone 12 Pro Max. Corrections and results.\n\nCorrection: the \"GPU ms per pass\" read from the Metal System Trace in my earlier comments is not GPU busy time. The Fragment interval includes time waiting on pacing, so it tracks the frame period (about 20 ms at 49 fps, 34 ms at 29 fps). Do not use it. The reliable measures are fps from a display-locked loop, and real GPU time per frame from a blocking `device.poll(Wait)` after submit (temporary switch, removed), read at the same thermal state. The thermal state itself (Serious for hours of testing) comes from the trace's `device-thermal-state-intervals`.\n\nReal GPU time per frame at Serious: default before this round 32.9 ms; skipping all SDF draws 1.2 ms (so the SDF draws are the whole frame); shaders with the original full core 34 ms; simple core pipeline 22 ms after the colourless shadows went; compact shader on every drawn primitive 12.5 ms.\n\nWhat the 20 ms was: `draw_shadow` emitted a shadow primitive for every card even when `box-shadow: none` made its colour transparent, and the shadow shader ran over the card's whole area plus blur margin (0.93 screens for the list card). Skipping colourless shadows alone took 32.9 to 22.2 ms. Within the simple pipeline, flat fill 3.4 ms; cost by stage: clip +3.4, affine +1.1, shape distance +8.0, fill colour +1.9, border +4.1. The cards use the Expressive theme's corner exponent 1.084 (not exactly circular), which kept them out of the compact shader until that shader learned convex superellipse corners with an exact shortcut outside the corner squares; that is the 22 to 12.5 ms step.\n\nLanded in the working tree: colourless shadow skip, compact shader (`CorePlain`), exact interior shortcut in `sd_shaped_rect`, backdrop as clear colour, plus the earlier simple pipeline. 248 blinc_gpu tests and 1483 layout, recorder, cn and app tests pass; a parity test compares the compact and core shaders pixel for pixel (within 2/255), including squircle corners.\n\nOpen: cool-phone numbers (the phone has been in Serious since long before these runs); shadows that do have colour still cost a pass over their blurred area (shadow shader switches); the retained scroll layer.","files":null}]}